{"type": "say", "text": "Thanks everyone for being here. As you know, the customer-notification service needs a production message-streaming platform before its next integration milestone, and we're down to two finalists: KafkaCloud and StreamGrid. Both passed our benchmark for 20,000 messages per second and sub-250-millisecond median delivery latency, so they're technically viable. The draft architecture currently recommends StreamGrid based on simpler operational setup and an initial capacity estimate of four brokers. Before I make a final decision, I want to hear from each of you on backend, support engineering, security, and FinOps perspectives. What concerns or constraints should I be aware of?"}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF1", "text": "I need to flag something important before we go further. While testing the notification client's retry behavior this morning, I confirmed that the service already has a KafkaCloud-compatible producer and consumer path. That means using KafkaCloud would require roughly two days of adapter cleanup, whereas moving to StreamGrid would require a new two-week client integration. I haven't updated the architecture document yet, so the current draft doesn't reflect this. Given our integration milestone timeline, this is a significant factor for the backend perspective."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF2", "text": "I need to flag something critical from a customer escalation yesterday. I confirmed that three enterprise customers require notifications for each account to remain in order. KafkaCloud can guarantee that ordering using a customer_id partition key, but StreamGrid's tested mode cannot. I have not yet added this requirement to the review ticket, so it's not reflected in the current architecture draft. This is a hard constraint from the support engineering perspective — if we can't guarantee per-account ordering, we risk violating commitments to those enterprise customers."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF4", "text": "I need to share a FinOps finding from this morning's cloud account reconciliation. We have existing KafkaCloud reserved-capacity credits that cover exactly six production brokers through the service's first year. StreamGrid has no equivalent credit. I should also note that the draft architecture's four-broker estimate hasn't been amended to reflect this — but given what Mei, Carlos, and Nadia have just raised, the capacity and cost picture may need to be revisited holistically before we finalize."}
```

